  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1131 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nTonight and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening shows a compact shortwave trough \nejecting NNE from Montana into Canada, with a belt of ascent \noverspreading Wyoming/western Nebraska into the Dakotas. A recent \nsurface analysis shows a stationary front linking up with the \nsurface low associated with the compact shortwave, extending from \nMontana through South Dakota and along the IA/MN border. We shower \nand storm activity has been exiting the area, pushing into northwest \nIowa after developing on the nose of the low-level jet. As for any \nfurther storm or shower development, we'll be waiting through the \nnight until westerly 700 mb winds increase convergence over eastern \nNebraska and western Iowa with scattered weak showers and a rumble \nor two of thunder anticipated after 5 AM, lasting through much of \nthe morning.\n\nAside from those rain chances, temperatures are slated to only fall \ninto the 70s this evening, limiting the relief from the heat that \nhas fully settled in for the next 7 days. Already, a Heat Advisory \nis in place for Butler to Pottawattamie Counties and points \nsouthward for heat indices that reached into the 100-107 range, and \nare salted to reach similar values tomorrow despite initial showers.\nClearing skies by noon will allow for plenty of time for the area to \nheat up. Shower and storm potential heading into the overnight will \nbe limited once again as the main forcing mechanisms remain west \nalong the High Plains and north of the forecast area where better \nLLJ convergence is anticipated to occur. \n\nFriday and Beyond:\n\nBy Friday, the shortwave mentioned above will have shed off through \nthe flow to the east of the persistent mid/upper low parked over the \nPacific Northwest, with little effect in terms of the day-to-day \nweather pattern. Stout ridging continues to hold over the area with \nmost of the convective action initiating up the High Plains or along \nthe quasi-stationary front over the Dakotas pointed southeast. We'll \nrely on established areas of convection to drift in from the west \n(carried by the weak southwesterly flow), or for storms to push into \nthe instability gradient from the north. Regardless, ensemble \nmembers paint a bleak picture for those needing meaningful \nrainfall through the next 7 days. \n\nHigh confidence continues to hold for heat stress across the area, \nwith high temperatures in the 90s remaining for much of the forecast \narea through Tuesday underneath the continued ridging aloft. Are \nthese temperatures the hottest we see compared to the summer months? \nWell not really, but the cumulative effect of highs 10-15 degrees \nabove normal for 7 days in a row and overnight lows failing to fall \nbelow 75 degrees for areas of southeast Nebraska into Iowa spell for \nMajor to Extreme Heat Risk. With a holiday weekend, fall sports, and \nother school-related activities back in full swing heading into\nthe weekend, this last gasp of summer will catch many off-\nguard. As far as Heat Advisories go, this is about as late as we\nhave ever issued one/had one take effect, further highlighting \njust how abnormal this is. Those waiting for a pattern break \nwill look towards the middle of next week where the main \nmid/upper low translates across the northern CONUS, dragging a \nfront through the area to knock temps back down into the 80s. \nFrom there, models are split as to the presence of another ridge\nbuilding to the west, or if additional rain chances keep the \nheat at bay towards the end of the week.",
